Zoca AI Front Desk Voice to Booking
Assignment

Build a focused, polished front-end experience for a salon's AI-powered front desk. This assignment
is about product judgment, interaction design, architecture, reliability, and how you work with AI - not
raw feature count.
Timebox 4 to 5 hours
Submit by Within 48 hours
Preferred stack React and TypeScript
AI use Claude or other AI tools are explicitly allowed

The scenario
A local salon uses Zoca to manage bookings. Its AI Front Desk handles customer calls, but an
operator should be able to understand what is happening and step in at any moment.
The incoming request: “Hi, I need a haircut and beard trim tomorrow evening. I prefer someone good
with fades. What is available?”
Your product should make this interaction feel calm, informed, and easy to recover when the
conversation or booking flow goes wrong.
What to build
Build one connected flow with the three parts below. Use realistic mock data and a simulated
backend where useful. Production telephony and speech-to-text are not required.
1 Live call console
● An incoming call state with caller details, relevant previous visits, and a visible recording or
privacy indicator.
● A credible audio waveform or playback progress indicator. A short supplied or self-created audio
clip is welcome; a simulated playback is also fine.
● A live transcript that progresses as the call plays, with speaker turns and a visible confidence
signal.
● AI-detected intent: requested services, preferred time, and stylist preference.
● Controls for Take Over, mute, speaker, and ending the call. The human handoff must feel
deliberate and clear.

2 AI booking recommendation
● Recommend 2 or 3 booking slots based on service duration, staff capability, and availability.
● Explain why the top recommendation is a good fit, rather than showing a black-box answer.
● Let the operator change the service, stylist, time, or deposit requirement before confirming.
● Handle a slot becoming unavailable while the operator is deciding.
3 Booking confirmation
● Confirm the appointment and optionally collect a deposit.
● Show a concise call summary and the customer-facing confirmation.
● Design failure states for payment failures, low transcript confidence, staff conflicts, and the caller
dropping before confirmation.

Audio and AI details
We care less about building speech-to-text and more about what the interface does with imperfect
information. Include at least one moment where the system needs human judgment. For example,
“fade” could be misheard as “facial”, and the operator should be able to see, correct, and safely
continue.
● Show how transcript confidence affects the booking flow.
● Make the audio state feel connected to the transcript rather than decorative.
● Avoid exposing secrets, raw customer data, or unsafe actions in the UI.
Design for real conditions
The happy path is only half the assignment. Make thoughtful choices for at least the following
conditions:
● Desktop and mobile layouts.
● Loading, empty, and error states.
● Slow or stale availability data.
● Keyboard focus and basic accessibility for the primary controls.
● A clear recovery path when the AI has low confidence or is wrong.
Working with AI
Use AI as part of the assignment if it helps you. We want to understand how you direct it, verify its
output, and retain your own product judgment.
Include an AI_NOTES.md file: Capture 3 to 5 prompts that materially helped, what you accepted and
edited, one suggestion you rejected and why, how you verified the work, and one edge case AI helped
surface.

